  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></math> is usually not considered as a transition element as the d-orbital electrons do not take part in reactions. This is why <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Zn</mi></math>&nbsp;exhibit&nbsp;only&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math> &nbsp;oxidation state.<br />But since, it is placed in the d-block along with other transition metals, it is technically a d-block element and a transition metal. Zinc has the lowest enthalpy of atomization among the first row transition metals.</p><table border=\"1\">\t<tbody>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>Elements</td>\t\t\t<td><math><mi>V</mi></math></td>\t\t\t<td><math><mi>C</mi><mi>u</mi></math></td>\t\t\t<td><math><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></math></td>\t\t\t<td><math><mi>F</mi><mi>e</mi></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td>Enthalpy of atomization</td>\t\t\t<td><math><mn>515</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></math></td>\t\t\t<td><math><mn>339</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></math></td>\t\t\t<td><math><mn>216</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></math></td>\t\t\t<td><math><mn>416</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t</tbody></table></blockquote>",
